      "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "ISO 12944 is the primary international standard for corrosion protection of steel structures by protective paint systems. It is a multi-part standard: Part 1 provides the general introduction and definitions; Part 2 classifies environments by corrosivity category (C1 very low through CX extreme for atmospheric environments, and Im1–Im4 for immersion environments); Part 3 covers design considerations; Part 5 specifies protective paint systems appropriate for each corrosivity category and durability class; Part 6 defines laboratory performance test methods; Parts 7, 8, and 9 address execution, development of specifications, and protective paint systems for offshore and related structures. Coating specifications based on ISO 12944 identify the corrosivity category of the environment, the required durability class (Low: up to 7 years, Medium: 7–15 years, High: 15–25 years, Very High: >25 years), and then reference Part 5 systems tables to select a compliant primer, intermediate coat, and topcoat system with specified minimum dry film thicknesses." }
